Chain-of-Thought prompt
● Now I am going to interview you. answer the questions from the perspective of a college student. walk
me through your thought process when you answer. First question: as a college student, what do you
think is the most important feature of a weather app?
○ As a college student, the most important feature of a weather app is being able to see accurate,
up‑to‑date weather at a glance.
○ 1. My day is chopped into small chunks.
■ Classes, walking across campus, grabbing food, study sessions — everything is in short
bursts. I don’t have time to dig through menus or interpret complicated charts. So I
immediately think: I need something fast and clear.
○ 2. Weather affects almost every transition.
■ If it’s raining, I need my jacket. If it’s hot, I need water. If it’s freezing, I need gloves.
So the app has to tell me the essentials instantly:
● current temp
● precipitation
● “feels like”
● any sudden changes coming soon
○ 3. College schedules change constantly.
■ Sometimes I’m running late or switching buildings. I don’t want to get caught in a
surprise downpour between classes. That makes real‑time updates and quick alerts
extremely important.
○ 4. I’m usually multitasking.
■ I’m checking the weather while walking, talking, or rushing. So the app needs to be
simple, readable, and fast.
● What are some features that existing weather apps do poorly, or even features that they are missing
entirely?
○ When I think about weather apps I’ve used — Apple Weather, Weather Channel, AccuWeather,
etc. — a few frustrations immediately come to mind. I’m usually checking the weather while
rushing between classes, so anything slow, cluttered, or unclear stands out fast.
1. Too Much Clutter on the Home Screen: I just want to know if I need a jacket before I sprint
across campus. Many apps bury the basics under ads, long scrolls, or random content like
“weather news” or “videos.” It slows me down.
2. Slow or Unreliable Real‑Time Updates: Weather on campus changes fast. I’ve had apps show
“clear skies” while it’s literally raining outside. Some apps refresh too slowly or don’t warn me
about sudden changes.
3. Poor Short‑Range Forecasting: I care more about the next hour than the next week. Many apps
focus on 7‑day forecasts but give vague or inaccurate minute‑by‑minute predictions.
4. Bad Notification Logic: Sometimes I get spammy alerts (“Rain expected tonight”) that aren’t
useful. Other times, I get no alert when a storm rolls in while I’m walking to class.
5. No Context for Students: College life is unique — lots of walking, lots of sudden schedule
changes. Weather apps don’t consider that.
